<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Rhodesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1965 CE – 1979 CE · capital Salisbury</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The unrecognised white-minority state declared by Ian Smith in 1965, which fought a long guerrilla war before giving way to Zimbabwe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the end of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> insisted on progress towards majority rule before independence. Rather than accept this, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-front">Rhodesian Front</a> government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a> issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unilateral-declaration-of-independence-rhodesia">Unilateral Declaration of Independence</a> on 11 November 1965, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Salisbury</a> as the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>No state recognised Rhodesia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, though trade continued through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>. A republic was declared in 1970 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clifford-dupont">Clifford Dupont</a> as president. From the late 1960s and especially after 1972, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">ZAPU</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-bush-war">Bush War</a>, known to nationalists as the second Chimurenga; tens of thousands died and white emigration grew.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under military and economic pressure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Smith</a> signed an Internal Settlement in March 1978 with moderate African leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-muzorewa">Abel Muzorewa</a>, and the state was renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zimbabwe-rhodesia">Zimbabwe Rhodesia</a> in June 1979.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rhodesia.jpg" alt="Rhodesia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Actarux (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhodesia_(1968%E2%80%931979).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1965 CE – 1979 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salisbury</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ian Smith, Clifford Dupont</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Shona, isiNdebele</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Rhodesia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=rhodesia" aria-label="Rhodesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5450 2936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rhodesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5770 3192L5812 3138M5770 3192L5842 3096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5812 3138h0M5842 3096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5770 3192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5770 3192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=rhodesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
